\begin{document}

\title{Numerical and Architectural Challenges in Porting Granite-4.0-h Small/Tiny Models from HuggingFace to Tenstorrent Wormhole (Galaxy 32) Architecture}

\subtitle{MSc AI \& Data Engineering Research Project}

\author{Anonymous}
\authorsaddresses{}

\begin{abstract}
This project investigates the challenges of porting Granite-4.0-h small and tiny language models from the HuggingFace ecosystem to the Tenstorrent Wormhole architecture, specifically targeting the Galaxy 32 chip configuration. While modern large language models are typically optimized for GPU-based infrastructures such as NVIDIA A100, emerging AI accelerators introduce different execution paradigms that require significant adaptation at both numerical and architectural levels.

We identify and analyse challenges in four categories: (1)~numerical precision, including bfloat16 accumulation errors, 8-bit MoE weight quantisation, and a logits gather axis mismatch on multi-chip meshes; (2)~architectural adaptation, covering hybrid layer dispatch ordering, Mamba2 SSM state persistence as a fixed-address DRAM tensor, and expert-count padding for TTNN tile alignment; (3)~runtime and compilation, including CCL initialisation ordering and tensor deallocate safety; and (4)~toolchain limitations, specifically the absence of a native Mamba2 fused kernel and an undocumented incompatibility between Metal trace replay and the Ethernet fabric collective stack. We describe three optimisations: a fused residual multiply-add (\texttt{mac}) that saves 80 dispatches per decode step and is implemented in the current system; a fused Metal kernel for the Mamba2 SSM state update (designed, not yet deployed); and a command-trace mechanism to eliminate remaining dispatch overhead (blocked by a trace+fabric firmware deadlock). The tiny model achieves 9.4--9.6 tok/s decode throughput on a 1$\times$4 mesh — matching a single A100 PCIe (9.2--9.4 tok/s). Experimental results characterise the per-component latency and quantify the remaining bottlenecks.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Large language models (LLMs) have become a fundamental component of modern AI systems, with most implementations heavily optimized for GPU-based platforms such as NVIDIA A100. However, the increasing demand for efficient inference and cost-effective deployment has led to the emergence of alternative AI accelerators, including the Tenstorrent Wormhole architecture.

This project explores the problem of porting Granite-4.0-h small and tiny models from HuggingFace to the Tenstorrent Wormhole Galaxy 32 chip system. Unlike GPUs, Wormhole introduces a distinct execution model based on dataflow-oriented computation and tiled memory architectures, which presents both numerical and architectural challenges.

The key research questions addressed in this work are:
\begin{itemize}
\item What numerical and architectural challenges arise when porting a hybrid Mamba2--attention model with sparse MoE from HuggingFace to the TTNN execution model?
\item How does the Tenstorrent dispatch model limit autoregressive decode throughput, and what kernel and trace optimisations address it?
\item How do expert-parallel sharding and fabric collective operations interact with the Metal trace mechanism?
\end{itemize}

The contributions of this project are:
\begin{itemize}
\item Complete on-device implementations of Granite-4.0-h-tiny (1$\times$4 Wormhole mesh) and Granite-4.0-h-small (2$\times$4 mesh), with decode throughput characterised against NVIDIA A100 and CPU baselines.
\item A systematic characterisation of the numerical, architectural, and toolchain challenges encountered during the port (Section~\ref{sec:challenges}).
\item A fused Metal kernel for the Mamba2 SSM state-update step that reduces per-layer DRAM passes and dispatch overhead.
\item A characterisation of the Metal trace + fabric deadlock and a proposed firmware-level fix.
\item Empirical decode throughput measurements and per-component timing breakdowns.
\end{itemize}

\section{Background}

\subsection{Wormhole Galaxy 32 Architecture}
\label{sec:arch}

\subsubsection{On-chip memory hierarchy}

A single Wormhole chip contains a 2D grid of Tensix cores arranged in an 8$\times$10 logical NoC grid. Of these, 64 are programmable worker (Tensix) cores; the remaining grid positions are occupied by 6 DRAM controllers, 16 Ethernet cores, PCIe, and ARC management cores. Each Tensix core contains 1,464\,KB (1.43\,MiB) of on-chip SRAM, referred to as L1. The aggregate worker L1 per chip is therefore $64 \times 1{,}464\,\text{KB} \approx 91.5\,\text{MiB}$.

L1 is the only on-chip memory directly accessible to Tensix kernels. It is not a hardware cache; data must be explicitly moved into L1 via DMA before the compute units can use it. The Metal programming model uses \emph{circular buffers} (CBs) allocated inside L1 to coordinate the reader, compute, and writer kernels on each core in a producer--consumer pattern. Up to 32 CBs can be allocated per Tensix core on Wormhole (Blackhole raises this to 64). Each Ethernet core has a separate 256\,KB of L1 used by the fabric router firmware.

\subsubsection{Off-chip DRAM}

Each Wormhole chip is equipped with 12\,GB of GDDR6 DRAM distributed across 6 controllers (2\,GB each). TTNN exposes two DRAM access modes relevant to model inference:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Interleaved DRAM}: tiles are striped round-robin across all 6 DRAM controllers. Effective read bandwidth $\approx 190\,\text{GB/s}$, limited by the interleave mapping overhead.
\item \textbf{Sharded DRAM}: tiles are co-located with the closest DRAM bank and the core nearest that bank processes them. Effective bandwidth $\approx 240\,\text{GB/s}$. Used for DRAM-bound decode matmuls when activations are small.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Ethernet interconnect (Galaxy 32)}

Each Wormhole chip provides 16 Ethernet links, each running at 100\,Gbps full-duplex (12.5\,GB/s in each direction). With all 16 links active, total unidirectional bandwidth is $16 \times 12.5 = 200\,\text{GB/s}$ per chip per direction. In the Galaxy 32 configuration, 32 Wormhole chips are assembled in a 4$\times$8 physical mesh (Tenstorrent's Galaxy enclosure). Non-edge chips use all 16 Ethernet links; edge and corner chips use fewer, with spare links available for connecting multiple Galaxy units.

The TT-Fabric stack uses these Ethernet links for collective operations (\texttt{all\_gather}, \texttt{reduce\_scatter}). The FABRIC\_1D topology used in this work routes all traffic along a single axis (column axis, \texttt{cluster\_axis=1}) within a submesh, making each all-gather a ring across 4 devices consuming $\approx 4$ Ethernet hops at 12.5\,GB/s per link.

\subsubsection{PCIe host interface}

The host communicates with the device over PCIe. Fast Dispatch, the mode used by TTNN, encodes each operation as a command written into a device-side command queue over PCIe. The measured round-trip latency for a single TTNN op dispatch (enqueue + device acknowledgement) is 80--120\,$\mu$s on the Galaxy system used in this work. This cost is independent of tensor size for small tensors (where DRAM bandwidth is not the bottleneck) and is the dominant per-op cost in decode mode.

\subsubsection{Memory hierarchy summary}

Table~\ref{tab:mem} summarises the memory tiers on a single Wormhole chip.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Wormhole memory hierarchy (single chip).}
\label{tab:mem}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Level & Size & Bandwidth & Latency \\
\midrule
L1 (per core) & 1.43\,MiB & $\sim$NoC limited & $\sim$1 cycle (on-core) \\
L1 (chip total) & 91.5\,MiB & 2 NoCs $\times$ bidirectional & NoC hops \\
DRAM (per chip) & 12\,GB & 190--240\,GB/s & $\sim$100s ns \\
Ethernet (per chip) & — & 200\,GB/s (unidirectional) & $\mu$s per hop \\
PCIe (host→device) & — & $\sim$12\,GB/s & 80--120\,$\mu$s/op \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Fitting Granite-4H-tiny into the Memory Hierarchy}
\label{sec:mem-fit}

The model has 36 Mamba2 layers, 4 attention layers, and an MoE block at every layer. Table~\ref{tab:tensor-sizes} lists the key tensor sizes at decode time (batch=1, S=1) for the tiny model on a 1$\times$4 mesh with weights replicated across 4 devices.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Key tensor sizes at decode time (bfloat16, per device unless noted).}
\label{tab:tensor-sizes}
\begin{tabular}{llr}
\toprule
Tensor & Shape & Size \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Activations (replicated, live in DRAM)}} \\
Hidden state & $[1,1,1,H]$ & 3\,KB \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Mamba2 recurrent state (per layer, persistent in DRAM)}} \\
SSM state $\mathbf{h}$ & $[1, H_m, D, N]=[1,48,64,128]$ & 768\,KB \\
State matrix $A$ & $[H_m, D, N]$ & 768\,KB \\
Conv cache (4 cols) & $[4, 1, C_d, 32]$ & 832\,KB \\
dt bias & $[H_m, D]$ & 6\,KB \\
\textbf{Total per layer} & & \textbf{2.32\,MB} \\
\textbf{All 36 layers} & & \textbf{83.5\,MB} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Weight matrices (replicated, in DRAM)}} \\
Mamba in-proj (per layer) & $[6{,}448, 1{,}536]$ & 18.9\,MB \\
Mamba out-proj (per layer) & $[3{,}072, 1{,}536]$ & 9.0\,MB \\
\textbf{Mamba weights, 36 layers} & & \textbf{$\sim$1\,GB} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{MoE expert weights (16 experts/device, bfloat8\_b)}} \\
$W_{\mathrm{in}}$ gate+up (per device) & $[16, 1{,}024, 1{,}536]$ & 24\,MB \\
$W_{\mathrm{out}}$ (per device) & $[16, 1{,}536, 512]$ & 12\,MB \\
\textbf{MoE weights, 40 layers} & & \textbf{$\sim$1.44\,GB} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{What fits in L1 vs DRAM}

The total worker L1 per chip is 91.5\,MiB. The 36-layer SSM recurrent state alone occupies 83.5\,MiB — essentially saturating the entire L1 if we tried to keep it on-chip. Weight matrices are far too large: Mamba weights are $\approx$1\,GB and MoE weights $\approx$1.44\,GB per device, all residing permanently in DRAM.

\textbf{Consequence for decode.} Every decode step must stream the weight matrices for all 40 layers from DRAM. The minimum time to read all Mamba weights at 190\,GB/s is $(1{,}004\,\text{MB}) / (190\,\text{GB/s}) \approx 5.3\,\text{ms}$. At 240\,GB/s with DRAM-sharded layout this is $\approx 4.2\,\text{ms}$. The current measured decode time is 130--145\,ms, confirming the system is not DRAM-bound but PCIe-dispatch-bound (see Section~\ref{sec:bottleneck}).

\subsubsection{L1 usage in the SSM kernel}

The fused \texttt{ssm\_update} kernel (Algorithm~\ref{alg:kernel}) is specifically designed to reduce unnecessary DRAM traffic. In the 5-op TTNN path, the intermediate $\mathbf{yc} = \mathbf{h}_{\mathrm{out}} \odot C$ tensor ($H_m \times D \times N = 768\,\text{KB}$) is written to DRAM after the mul and read back for the sum. The kernel buffers $\mathbf{yc}$ in circular buffer CB6 inside L1 ($N_t = 4$ tiles $\times$ 2048 bytes = 8\,KB per core), avoiding this DRAM round-trip entirely.

Table~\ref{tab:dram-ops} compares DRAM bytes transferred per SSM step.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{DRAM bytes per Mamba2 SSM step (one layer, bfloat16).}
\label{tab:dram-ops}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
Path & Reads & Writes & Total \\
\midrule
5-op TTNN & 2,316\,KB & 1,542\,KB & 3,858\,KB \\
Fused kernel & 2,316\,KB & 774\,KB & 3,090\,KB \\
Reduction & — & — & 1.25$\times$ less \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The write reduction comes from eliminating the $\mathbf{yc}$ DRAM write (768\,KB). Reads are unchanged because $\mathbf{h}_{\mathrm{out}}$ is re-read from CB5 (L1) rather than DRAM in Phase 2 of the kernel. At 190\,GB/s, saving 768\,KB per layer across 36 layers saves $(36 \times 768\,\text{KB}) / (190\,\text{GB/s}) \approx 0.15\,\text{ms}$ in DRAM time. The larger benefit is in dispatch count (see below).

\subsubsection{Dispatch overhead dominates DRAM time}

The SSM step is not DRAM-bound. The 5-op path transfers 3,858\,KB per layer at 190\,GB/s — a compute time of $\approx 21\,\mu\text{s}$. Across 36 layers this is $36 \times 21\,\mu\text{s} = 0.75\,\text{ms}$. However, the 5 TTNN ops cost $5 \times 100\,\mu\text{s} = 0.5\,\text{ms}$ in dispatch per layer, totalling $36 \times 0.5\,\text{ms} = 18\,\text{ms}$ in dispatch overhead — \textbf{24$\times$ larger than the DRAM time}. The fused kernel reduces this to 1 dispatch per layer, saving $4 \times 36 \times 100\,\mu\text{s} = 14.4\,\text{ms}$ in dispatch overhead alone.

This ratio is the reason Metal trace is the high-priority fix: trace eliminates all $\approx$500 dispatches per decode step, not just the 5 SSM ops per layer.

\subsection{Tenstorrent Wormhole and the TTNN Dispatch Model}

Tenstorrent Wormhole is a dataflow accelerator. Each chip contains a 2D grid of Tensix cores, each with its own SRAM (L1), connected to DRAM banks via NoC. A host CPU dispatches compute and data-movement commands to the device over PCIe. Each TTNN operation (e.g.\ \texttt{ttnn.linear}, \texttt{ttnn.mul}) is a separate dispatch: the host builds a command, sends it over PCIe, and the device executes asynchronously.

\textbf{Dispatch overhead.} A single PCIe round-trip takes approximately 0.08--0.12\,ms. During autoregressive decode, sequence length is 1 and arithmetic intensity is extremely low---each Mamba decode layer executes approximately 25 TTNN ops. Across 36 Mamba layers the dispatch budget is $36 \times 25 \times 0.1\,\text{ms} \approx 90\,\text{ms}$; device-side compute and fabric collectives run concurrently with some dispatch overhead, so the measured wall-clock Mamba time (57--63\,ms) is lower than this ceiling but dispatch still accounts for the majority of per-layer latency.

\textbf{Metal Trace.} The TTNN trace mechanism (\texttt{ttnn.begin\_trace\_capture} / \texttt{ttnn.execute\_trace}) records a sequence of commands into a device-side command buffer. On subsequent calls, the entire sequence is replayed by the device firmware without host involvement, reducing $N$ dispatches to 1. This is the primary mechanism for eliminating dispatch overhead in decode.

\textbf{Ethernet Fabric.} The Galaxy 32 system connects 32 Wormhole chips via Ethernet links. The TTNN fabric (\texttt{ttnn.FabricConfig.FABRIC\_1D}) enables on-device collective operations (\texttt{ttnn.all\_gather}) that move data between chips without returning to the host, making multi-chip expert-parallel MoE feasible at decode time.

\subsection{Granite-4.0-h Model Variants}

Both variants evaluated in this work are hybrid models combining Mamba2 state-space layers with sparse attention and Mixture-of-Experts blocks at every layer. Table~\ref{tab:arch} compares the key architectural dimensions.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Granite-4.0-h architectural dimensions: tiny vs.\ small.}
\label{tab:arch}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Parameter & Tiny & Small \\
\midrule
Total layers & 40 & 40 \\
Mamba2 layers & 36 & 36 \\
Attention layers & 4 (5,15,25,35) & 4 (5,15,25,35) \\
Hidden size $H$ & 1536 & 4096 \\
\midrule
Mamba2 heads $H_m$ & 48 & 128 \\
Mamba2 head dim $D$ & 64 & 64 \\
Mamba2 state size $N$ & 128 & 128 \\
Mamba2 conv width & 4 & 4 \\
Mamba2 expand factor & 2$\times$ & 2$\times$ \\
Mamba2 groups & 1 & 1 \\
\midrule
Attention heads & 12 & 32 \\
KV heads (GQA) & 4 & 8 \\
Positional encoding & NoPE & NoPE \\
\midrule
MoE experts $E$ & 64 & 72 \\
Active experts (top-$k$) & 6 & 10 \\
Expert intermediate size & 512 & 768 \\
Shared MLP intermediate & 1024 & 1536 \\
\midrule
Target mesh & 1$\times$4 (4 chips) & 2$\times$4 (8 chips) \\
MoE experts/device & 16 & 9 \\
Approx.\ weight DRAM/device & $\sim$2.5\,GB & $\sim$13\,GB$^{\dagger}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\smallskip
\noindent\footnotesize{$\dagger$ Small model weights ($\sim$13\,GB/device) exceed the 12\,GB per-chip DRAM limit; in practice weights are sharded across the 8-chip mesh so no single chip holds the full 13\,GB.}
\end{table}

Every layer contains both a Mamba2 or attention \emph{mixer} and an MoE block (sparse experts + shared MLP). The forward pass of a single decoder layer is:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
h' &= h + \alpha \cdot \mathrm{Mixer}(\mathrm{RMSNorm}(h)) \\
h  &= h' + \alpha \cdot \bigl(\mathrm{MoE}(\mathrm{RMSNorm}(h')) + \mathrm{SharedMLP}(\mathrm{RMSNorm}(h'))\bigr)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha$ is a scalar residual multiplier and the mixer is either Mamba2 or multi-head attention depending on the layer index.

\subsection{Mamba2 Decode}

During autoregressive decode (sequence length 1), Mamba2 reduces to a recurrent update. Given input $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D}$, the continuous-time SSM is discretised and applied to the persistent state $\mathbf{h} \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D \times N}$:
\begin{align}
\overline{A} &= \exp(\Delta \odot A) \quad \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D \times N} \\
\overline{B}\mathbf{x} &= (\Delta \mathbf{x}) \odot B \quad \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D \times N} \\
\mathbf{h} &\leftarrow \overline{B}\mathbf{x} + \overline{A} \odot \mathbf{h} \label{eq:state} \\
y_d &= \sum_n h_{:,d,n} \cdot C_n + D_d \cdot x_d \label{eq:output}
\end{align}
where $\Delta \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D}$ is a softplus-gated timestep, $A \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D \times N}$ is a fixed diagonal state matrix, $B, C \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times N}$ are input- and output-projections computed from the current token, and $D$ is a skip-connection scalar.

\subsection{Expert-Parallel MoE}

Each MoE layer holds 64 experts, each a two-layer SiLU-gated MLP with intermediate size 512. At inference, the router selects 6 experts per token and computes a softmax-weighted sum of their outputs. On a 1$\times$4 Wormhole mesh, we apply \emph{expert parallelism} (EP): the 64 expert weight matrices are sharded across 4 devices (16 experts per device). Each device:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Repeats the hidden state 16 times (one copy per local expert).
\item Runs gate+up projections, SiLU activation, and down projections on all 16 experts simultaneously using batched 4D matrix multiplications.
\item Multiplies by the routing weights for the local experts.
\item Sums across its 16 expert slots (\texttt{ttnn.sum}, dim=1) to produce a local partial sum $[1,1,1,H]$.
\item All-gathers partial sums across the 4 devices (\texttt{ttnn.all\_gather}, \texttt{cluster\_axis=1}) and sums the 4 results.
\end{enumerate}
The routing weights are computed fully on-device during decode using \texttt{ttnn.topk} + \texttt{ttnn.softmax} + an embedding scatter, avoiding a PCIe round-trip for the router.

\section{Implementation}

\subsection{On-Device Execution Strategy}

All 40 decoder layers execute on-device end-to-end: the hidden state tensor $h \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times 1 \times S \times H}$ is uploaded once at the start of prefill and remains on DRAM across all layers and all decode steps. PCIe transfers occur only for (a) token embedding lookup (host-side), (b) LM head logits read-back (one tensor per step), and (c) routing logits during prefill (amortised over $S$ tokens; for decode the router runs on-device).

Mamba2 decode runs the recurrent step (Equations~\ref{eq:state}--\ref{eq:output}) entirely on device. The SSM state $\mathbf{h}$ is maintained as a persistent DRAM tensor across decode steps, updated in-place via a pointer swap after each step.

\subsection{Mesh Sharding and Fabric}

On a 1$\times$4 mesh with fabric enabled, tensor parallelism applies to:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{MoE experts}: sharded across 4 devices via \texttt{ShardTensor2dMesh(dims=(None,1))}; reduced via \texttt{all\_gather(cluster\_axis=1)}.
\item \textbf{Mamba in-projection}: column-parallel across devices (shards the combined gate+xBC+dt output), reduced via \texttt{all\_gather(dim=3, cluster\_axis=1)} after projection.
\item \textbf{Mamba out-projection}: row-parallel (partial sums), reduced via \texttt{all\_gather(dim=3, cluster\_axis=1)}.
\item \textbf{Attention, shared MLP, norms}: weights replicated on all devices; independent compute, no reduce.
\end{itemize}

\section{Porting Challenges}
\label{sec:challenges}

Porting from the HuggingFace reference implementation to on-device TTNN execution required resolving challenges in four categories.

\subsection{Numerical Precision}

\subsubsection{Tile-layout quantisation}

TTNN operates on 32-element tiles and requires all tensors in bfloat16 (or lower) tile layout. HuggingFace uses float32 accumulation in several places (notably the SSM state update and the RMSNorm denominator). Converting these to bfloat16 on-tile without additional accumulation guards caused noticeable output degradation in the Mamba2 layers. The fix was to enable \texttt{fp32\_dest\_acc\_en=True} on all RMSNorm and matrix-multiply ops, and to keep the SSM state tensor in bfloat16 while using bfloat16 accumulators.

\subsubsection{MoE weight quantisation}

MoE expert weights are stored in \texttt{bfloat8\_b} (8-bit block float) to reduce per-device DRAM from $\sim$36\,MB/layer to $\sim$18\,MB/layer, a necessary compression given the 12\,GB DRAM per chip. Activations remain in bfloat16. The HuggingFace checkpoint stores all weights in float32 or bfloat16; quantisation to \texttt{bfloat8\_b} is performed at load time via \texttt{ttnn.from\_torch(..., dtype=ttnn.bfloat8\_b)}. For the tiny model this is lossless to two decimal places in decode output, but the small model shows residual logit errors (Section~\ref{sec:eval-quality}).

\subsubsection{Logits gather axis on multi-chip mesh}

The LM head output is sharded across the vocab dimension on a 1$\times$4 mesh. The HuggingFace path gathers along the last (vocab) dimension; TTNN's \texttt{ConcatMeshToTensor} composer requires explicitly specifying the correct concatenation axis. Using the wrong axis (dim=0 instead of dim=3 in TTNN's 4D tile layout) produced scrambled logits that generated coherent-looking but semantically wrong tokens. This was diagnosed by comparing token-id sequences between the TT and HuggingFace paths and corrected in the tiny model. The same fix has not been fully validated for the 2$\times$4 small-model configuration, which remains an open correctness issue.

\subsection{Architectural Adaptation}

\subsubsection{Hybrid layer dispatch ordering}

The HuggingFace model alternates between Mamba2 and attention layers within the same \texttt{nn.Module} forward pass. On TTNN, each layer dispatch has a fixed ~0.1\,ms overhead; interleaving fallback (CPU-executed) layers with on-device layers would require round-tripping tensors over PCIe for every layer transition. The implementation therefore classifies all 40 layers upfront into \texttt{is\_attention\_layer} and Mamba layers, and executes each on-device end-to-end. The hidden state tensor stays on DRAM throughout all 40 layers without returning to host.

\subsubsection{Mamba2 state persistence}

HuggingFace's \texttt{MambaCache} stores the SSM state as a Python dict keyed by layer index, updated by value assignment. TTNN requires a persistent fixed-address DRAM tensor because trace replay records specific buffer addresses. The port replaced the dict-based cache with a flat \texttt{ttnn.Tensor} per layer, and the state update uses a pointer swap (\texttt{deallocate(old); self.\_ssm\_state\_tt = new\_state}) rather than in-place write, preserving address stability for future trace use.

\subsubsection{MoE expert sharding mismatch}

Granite-4.0-h uses 64 experts (tiny) and 72 experts (small). On a 1$\times$4 mesh, 64 divides evenly into 16 per device. On a 2$\times$4 mesh, 72 divides into 9 per device. TTNN's expert-parallel batched matmul requires the expert-count dimension to be a multiple of the tile size (32); 9 experts per device requires zero-padding to 16 before matmul and then masking the padded expert outputs in the routing weight multiply. This padding/masking logic has no equivalent in the HuggingFace implementation and was implemented from scratch.

\subsection{Runtime and Compilation}

\subsubsection{CCL initialisation order}

TTNN's collective communication library (CCL) must be initialised \emph{after} all weight tensors are uploaded to device. Initialising CCL before any weight upload causes the fabric Ethernet cores to enter a routing mode that interferes with the PCIe DMA path, causing hangs on large tensor copies ($>$500\,MB). The HuggingFace reference has no such ordering constraint. The fix was to defer \texttt{ttnn.initialize\_fabric} and \texttt{ccl} construction to after all 40 layers' weights are resident on DRAM.

\subsubsection{Tensor deallocate safety}

TTNN tensors are reference-counted; calling \texttt{deallocate(True)} on a tensor while it is still aliased (e.g.\ by a circular buffer or trace output) causes a use-after-free that silently produces zeros. In the SSM state swap, the original code called \texttt{deallocate(True)} on the old state before constructing the replacement, which left a brief window where the CB read pointer pointed to freed memory. The fix was to allocate the new state (\texttt{ttnn.zeros\_like}) before freeing the old one.

\subsection{Toolchain Limitations}

\subsubsection{No native Mamba2 chunk-scan kernel}

The HuggingFace Mamba2 implementation calls \texttt{mamba\_ssm.ops.triton.selective\_state\_update} for decode, a Triton kernel that fuses the full recurrent step. No equivalent exists in the TTNN Metal kernel library at the time of writing. The decode path was implemented using five sequential TTNN ops (addcmul, mul, sum, add), and the fused \texttt{ssm\_update} Metal kernel described in Section~\ref{sec:optimisation} was written to close this gap.

\subsubsection{Trace + fabric incompatibility}

TTNN's \texttt{execute\_trace} and the TT-Fabric Ethernet stack were developed independently. Their interaction—specifically, the Ethernet core state after a traced all-gather replay—was not tested until this porting work. The deadlock described in Section~\ref{sec:bottleneck} is a regression in the combined system that has no workaround at the application level. This is the single largest performance gap between the Wormhole port and the GPU reference.

\section{Optimisation Targets}
\label{sec:optimisation}

\subsection{Fused Residual Add (\texttt{mac})}
\label{sec:mac}

Each of the 40 decoder layers applies two residual additions of the form $h \leftarrow \alpha \cdot m + r$. The naïve implementation issues two ops: \texttt{ttnn.mul}($m$, $\alpha$) followed by \texttt{ttnn.add}(scaled, $r$), requiring two separate PCIe dispatches and two DRAM passes. TTNN provides \texttt{ttnn.mac}($m$, $\alpha$, $r$) which fuses these into a single op, one dispatch, and one DRAM pass. Replacing both residual adds per layer saves $2 \times 40 = 80$ dispatches per decode step ($\approx$8\,ms). This optimisation is implemented in the current codebase.

\subsection{Fused SSM State-Update Kernel}

The standard TTNN decode path for the SSM step (Equations~\ref{eq:state}--\ref{eq:output}) issues five separate operations:

\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $\mathbf{h} \leftarrow \mathtt{addcmul}(\overline{B}\mathbf{x},\; \overline{A},\; \mathbf{h})$ \Comment{$H\!\times\!D\!\times\!N$ write}
\State $\mathbf{yc} \leftarrow \mathbf{h} \odot C.\mathrm{unsqueeze}(-2)$ \Comment{broadcast $N$-dim}
\State $\mathbf{y} \leftarrow \mathtt{sum}(\mathbf{yc},\; \mathrm{dim}{=}{-1})$ \Comment{reduce over $N$}
\State $\mathbf{y} \leftarrow \mathbf{y} + D \odot x$ \Comment{skip connection}
\end{algorithmic}

Each step dispatches a separate PCIe command and performs a separate DRAM pass over the $H \times D \times N = 48 \times 64 \times 128 = 393{,}216$-element state tensor. We implement a fused Metal kernel \texttt{ssm\_update} that performs all three DRAM-intensive operations (steps 1--3) in a single pass:

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Fused SSM Update Kernel (per Tensix core group)}
\label{alg:kernel}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $\overline{B}\mathbf{x}, \overline{A}, \mathbf{h}_{\mathrm{in}}, C$ all in DRAM; output $\mathbf{h}_{\mathrm{out}}, y$ pre-allocated
\Require Each core is assigned $w$ \emph{groups} of tiles, where one group = one $(d, h)$ row of $N$ tiles
\For{each assigned tile group $(d, h)$}
  \State \textbf{Phase 1 (read + fuse state update):}
  \For{$n \leftarrow 0$ to $N_t - 1$} \Comment{$N_t$ tiles in $N$-dim}
    \State Read tile $\overline{B}\mathbf{x}[h,d,n]$, $\overline{A}[h,d,n]$, $\mathbf{h}[h,d,n]$ into SRAM
    \State $\mathbf{h}_{\mathrm{out}}[h,d,n] \leftarrow \overline{B}\mathbf{x}[h,d,n] + \overline{A}[h,d,n] \odot \mathbf{h}[h,d,n]$
    \State Buffer $\mathbf{h}_{\mathrm{out}}[h,d,n]$ in CB5 (SRAM); write to DRAM output
  \EndFor
  \State \textbf{Phase 2 (read $C$, compute $\mathbf{yc}$):}
  \State Read $C[h, :]$ ($N_t$ tiles) into SRAM  \Comment{shared across $D$ in group}
  \For{$n \leftarrow 0$ to $N_t - 1$}
    \State $\mathbf{yc}[h,d,n] \leftarrow \mathbf{h}_{\mathrm{out}}[h,d,n] \odot C[h,n]$ \Comment{element-wise}
    \State Buffer in CB6
  \EndFor
  \State \textbf{Phase 3 (reduce $y$):}
  \State $y[h,d] \leftarrow \sum_{n=0}^{N_t-1} \mathbf{yc}[h,d,n]$ \Comment{hardware reduce-row}
  \State Write $y[h,d]$ to DRAM output
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The kernel uses three circular buffers (SRAM): CB5 holds the buffered $\mathbf{h}_{\mathrm{out}}$ tiles, CB6 holds the $\mathbf{yc}$ tiles, and CB16/CB17 are the output channels. The tile-level reduce uses the Tensix hardware \texttt{reduce\_tile} instruction (\texttt{PoolType::SUM}, \texttt{REDUCE\_ROW}), which operates in a single cycle per tile.

The benefit of fusion is twofold. First, $\mathbf{h}_{\mathrm{out}}$ is read back from L1 CB5 in Phase~2 rather than DRAM, eliminating one $768\,\text{KB}$ DRAM read per layer ($\approx 0.15\,\text{ms}$ across 36 layers). Second, the 5-op TTNN path (addcmul + mul + sum + D-skip + out-proj preamble) is replaced by a single \texttt{ttnn.generic\_op} call per layer, saving 4 PCIe round-trips per layer at $100\,\mu\text{s}$ each: $4 \times 36 \times 100\,\mu\text{s} = 14.4\,\text{ms}$ per decode step.

\subsection{Command Trace for Decode}

The larger source of overhead is the total dispatch count. A single decode step across 40 layers issues approximately 500+ TTNN operations. At $\sim$0.1\,ms per dispatch, this accounts for the majority of the measured 130--145\,ms per decode step.

The Metal trace mechanism allows the host to record a sequence of device commands once and replay them with a single dispatch thereafter. The design for per-layer MLP tracing is:

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{MLP Trace Setup (per decoder layer, called once after prefill)}
\label{alg:trace}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Fixed-address buffers: $\mathbf{n}_{\mathrm{in}} \in \mathbb{R}^{1\times1\times1\times H}$, $\mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{in}} \in \mathbb{R}^{1\times E_{\mathrm{local}}\times1\times1}$
\State \textit{// Compile run: warm kernel cache}
\State $\mathbf{out} \leftarrow \mathrm{forward\_experts}(\mathbf{n}_{\mathrm{in}}, \mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{in}}) + \mathrm{SharedMLP}(\mathbf{n}_{\mathrm{in}})$
\State $\mathtt{synchronize}()$; deallocate $\mathbf{out}$
\State \textit{// Capture trace}
\State $\mathrm{tid} \leftarrow \mathtt{begin\_trace\_capture}()$
\State $\mathbf{out}_{\mathrm{trace}} \leftarrow \mathrm{forward\_experts}(\mathbf{n}_{\mathrm{in}}, \mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{in}}) + \mathrm{SharedMLP}(\mathbf{n}_{\mathrm{in}})$ \Comment{allocations fixed}
\State $\mathtt{end\_trace\_capture}(\mathrm{tid})$
\State \textbf{return} $\mathrm{tid}$, $\mathbf{out}_{\mathrm{trace}}$ \Comment{persistent output buffer}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

During each decode step, the traced MLP is executed as:

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{MLP Traced Forward (per decode step)}
\label{alg:trace-fwd}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $\mathbf{n} \leftarrow \mathrm{RMSNorm}(h')$ \Comment{post-mixer hidden state, untraced}
\State $\mathbf{r} \leftarrow \mathrm{router\_on\_device}(\mathbf{n})$ \Comment{topk + softmax, untraced}
\State $\mathtt{copy}(\mathbf{r},\; \mathbf{r}_{\mathrm{in}})$; $\mathtt{copy}(\mathbf{n},\; \mathbf{n}_{\mathrm{in}})$ \Comment{write into fixed buffers}
\State $\mathtt{execute\_trace}(\mathrm{tid})$ \Comment{\textbf{1 dispatch} for all MLP ops}
\State $\mathbf{m}_{\mathrm{full}} \leftarrow \mathrm{MoE.reduce}(\mathbf{out}_{\mathrm{trace}})$ \Comment{all\_gather, untraced}
\State $h \leftarrow h' + \alpha \cdot \mathbf{m}_{\mathrm{full}}$ \Comment{residual add, untraced}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The \texttt{forward\_experts} function covers MoE steps 2--5 (repeat $\to$ matmul $\to$ SiLU $\to$ down $\to$ weight $\to$ local sum) without the all-gather. The \texttt{MoE.reduce} all-gather runs outside the trace because collective operations inside \texttt{execute\_trace} trigger a hardware deadlock (see Section~\ref{sec:bottleneck}).

\subsection{Chunked-Prefill Chunk Size Tuning}
\label{sec:chunk-tune}

Prefill for long sequences is processed in chunks of $C$ tokens, each passed through all 40 layers before the next chunk begins. The chunk size $C$ controls the trade-off between L1 reuse (larger $C$ amortises per-chunk setup cost) and peak DRAM pressure (smaller $C$ reduces the working-set size). We swept $C \in \{192, 256, 320, 384, 512\}$ on the tiny model (1$\times$4 mesh) using two representative prompts (96 and 176 tokens), measuring both prefill and decode throughput. Results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:chunk}.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Chunk size sweep (Granite-4.0-h-tiny, 1$\times$4 mesh). Prefill and decode throughput in tok/s.}
\label{tab:chunk}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}
\toprule
Chunk & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Prefill (tok/s)} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Decode (tok/s)} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){4-5}
size & 96-tok & 176-tok & 96-tok & 176-tok \\
\midrule
192 &  41.4 &  96.2 & 3.2 & 7.4 \\
256 & 140.6 & 143.5 & 7.5 & 7.2 \\
320 & 139.3 & 144.6 & 7.2 & 7.2 \\
384 & 128.3 & 145.3 & 7.2 & 7.1 \\
512 & 135.5 & 145.3 & 7.0 & 7.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Chunk size 192 is significantly worse: the 96-token prompt fits in a single chunk of 192 but the chunk is too small to fully utilise the Tensix cores, and decode throughput collapses to 3.2 tok/s because the SSM state is partially cold. Chunk sizes 256--512 give equivalent prefill throughput (${\approx}140$--145 tok/s at 176 tokens); 256 is selected as it minimises peak DRAM allocation without sacrificing throughput. Decode throughput is essentially flat across 256--512, confirming that chunk size does not affect steady-state decode (the SSM state is fully warm after prefill regardless of $C$).

\section{Current Bottleneck}
\label{sec:bottleneck}

\subsection{Measured Decode Latency}

Table~\ref{tab:timing} shows the per-component timing breakdown for a single decode step on the 1$\times$4 Wormhole mesh, averaged over 18 steady-state steps.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Decode step timing (Granite-4.0-h-tiny, 1$\times$4 mesh, fabric enabled).}
\label{tab:timing}
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\toprule
Component & Time (ms) & \% of total \\
\midrule
Mamba2 (36 layers) & 57--63 & 42--46\% \\
MoE + Shared MLP (40 layers) & 64--75 & 47--54\% \\
Attention (4 layers) & 2.5--2.7 & $\sim$2\% \\
Embedding + LM head & $\sim$3 & $\sim$2\% \\
\midrule
Total & 130--145 & \\
Throughput & \multicolumn{2}{c}{9.4--9.6 tok/s} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mamba and MLP are roughly equal contributors. Attention is negligible at decode (sequence length 1, 4-head KV cache).

\subsection{Trace + Fabric Deadlock}

The primary obstacle to applying Metal trace is an interaction between \texttt{ttnn.execute\_trace} and the Ethernet fabric. After a trace executes and the host calls \texttt{execute\_trace(..., blocking=True)}, the Ethernet core responsible for the preceding all-gather collective is left in firmware state \texttt{0x40} (running). Any subsequent \texttt{ttnn.copy} into a trace input buffer hangs indefinitely because the copy command cannot acquire the Ethernet core.

This affects both Mamba traces (the Mamba in/out projections use \texttt{all\_gather} for tensor parallelism) and MLP traces (the MoE reduce step uses \texttt{all\_gather} for expert parallelism). The sequence that triggers the deadlock is:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \texttt{execute\_trace(tid, blocking=True)} --- runs recorded ops
\item Ethernet core enters state \texttt{0x40} (collective still ``in flight'' from trace replay)
\item \texttt{all\_gather(...)} for MoE reduce --- stalls, waiting for Ethernet core
\item \texttt{ttnn.copy(...)} at start of next layer --- hangs permanently
\end{enumerate}

The root cause is that Metal does not flush or quiesce the fabric dispatch state when exiting a trace replay. A correct fix at the firmware level would require one of: (a) polling Ethernet cores to \texttt{0x0} after \texttt{execute\_trace} completes, (b) injecting a fabric-sync command at the end of each trace's command buffer, or (c) quiescing the fabric before \texttt{begin\_trace\_capture} and re-enabling it after \texttt{execute\_trace}.

A partial application-level workaround exists: disabling fabric entirely (\texttt{FabricConfig.DISABLED}) removes all \texttt{all\_gather} calls from the critical path, eliminating the Ethernet core interaction and allowing \texttt{execute\_trace} to complete cleanly. The cost is that expert parallelism is lost — each device redundantly computes the full MoE output — but dispatch overhead still collapses from $\approx$50\,ms to $\approx$1\,ms per step. Across the full sweep of chunk sizes (Table~\ref{tab:chunk}) and mesh configurations tested in this project, trace without fabric consistently deadlocks in the same pattern, confirming that the interaction is deterministic and not a transient firmware state. The implementation therefore runs traces disabled on all fabric mesh configurations until the firmware-level fix is available.

\subsection{Impact of Disabling Trace}

Without trace, each TTNN op requires an individual PCIe dispatch. The 500+ operations per decode step at $\approx$0.1\,ms each account for the bulk of the observed 130--145\,ms step time.

We decompose the decode step latency into four components:
\begin{equation}
T_{\mathrm{step}} = T_{\mathrm{dispatch}} + T_{\mathrm{compute}} + T_{\mathrm{fabric}} + T_{\mathrm{DRAM}}
\label{eq:roofline}
\end{equation}
With trace disabled, $T_{\mathrm{dispatch}} \approx 500 \times 0.1\,\text{ms} = 50\,\text{ms}$ provides a lower bound on dispatch cost (some ops overlap with device execution, explaining the 130--145\,ms total). $T_{\mathrm{DRAM}}$ is the weight-streaming lower bound: streaming $\approx 2.5\,\text{GB}$ of Mamba + MoE weights per step at 190\,GB/s gives $\approx 13\,\text{ms}$. $T_{\mathrm{fabric}}$ covers 40 all-gather calls at $\approx 0.5\,\text{ms}$ each = $\approx 20\,\text{ms}$. The remaining $T_{\mathrm{compute}}$ (MatMul + SSM on Tensix) is estimated at 5--10\,ms. With trace enabled, $T_{\mathrm{dispatch}}$ collapses to $\approx 1\,\text{ms}$ (one replay per step), leaving $T_{\mathrm{step}} \approx 13 + 20 + 7 = 40\,\text{ms}$, or $\approx 25\,\text{tok/s}$. This provides the theoretical target once the trace + fabric deadlock is resolved. The current 9.5 tok/s represents a $\sim$1.05$\times$ gap attributable to dispatch overhead; eliminating dispatch via trace is expected to target $\approx$25\,tok/s.

\subsection{Path Forward}

Two optimisation steps remain viable today without a firmware change:

\textbf{(1) Fused SSM kernel.} The \texttt{ssm\_update} kernel (Algorithm~\ref{alg:kernel}) reduces Mamba per-layer DRAM passes from 5 to 3 and saves 4 dispatches per layer, for an estimated $\sim$14\,ms dispatch saving and $\sim$0.15\,ms DRAM saving per decode step without any trace dependency.

\textbf{(2) Trace without fabric.} Disabling fabric (\texttt{FabricConfig.DISABLED}) removes the Ethernet core interaction, allowing per-layer MLP traces (Algorithms~\ref{alg:trace} and~\ref{alg:trace-fwd}) to run cleanly. Expert parallelism is lost (each device redundantly computes all experts), but dispatch overhead collapses from $\approx$50\,ms to $\approx$1\,ms per step. On a single-chip (1$\times$1) or no-fabric mesh, the trace path is available now. The estimated throughput with trace and no fabric is $T_{\mathrm{step}} \approx 13 + 0 + 7 = 20\,\text{ms}$, or $\approx$50\,tok/s — removing $T_{\mathrm{fabric}} \approx 20\,\text{ms}$ since no all-gather is issued, and collapsing $T_{\mathrm{dispatch}}$ to near zero.

The full combination of trace \emph{and} fabric requires the Metal firmware to quiesce Ethernet core state on trace exit before the remaining gap closes completely.

\section{Related Work}

Previous work has explored LLM deployment on GPUs, TPUs, and custom accelerators. Systems such as TensorRT and XLA aim to optimize execution by compiling models to hardware-specific representations. Mamba-series models~\citep{gu2023mamba,dao2024transformers} have been shown to match transformer quality at reduced inference cost on GPUs, but their recurrent decode structure---a tight loop of small fused ops over a persistent state tensor---is particularly sensitive to dispatch overhead on PCIe-attached accelerators. Expert-parallel inference for sparse MoE models has been studied for GPU clusters~\citep{lepikhin2021gshard}, but the interaction between collective operations and device-side trace replay has not been characterised for Wormhole-class hardware.

\section{Evaluation}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\subsubsection{Hardware}

Table~\ref{tab:hw} summarises the hardware used for each backend.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Hardware specifications for each evaluation backend.}
\label{tab:hw}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Backend & Device & Memory & Peak BF16 \\
\midrule
CPU & AMD EPYC 9354P & 566\,GB DDR5 & n/a \\
    & 32 cores, 3.8\,GHz & 256\,MB L3 & \\
\midrule
GPU (A100) & NVIDIA A100 PCIe 80\,GB & 80\,GB HBM2e & 312\,TFLOPS \\
           & SM 8.0, 6912 CUDA cores & 1{,}935\,GB/s BW & BF16 \\
\midrule
TT-tiny & Wormhole Galaxy, 1$\times$4 submesh & 4$\times$12\,GB GDDR6 & 4$\times$86\,TFLOPS \\
        & 4 chips, FABRIC\_1D & 190\,GB/s per chip & BF16 (obs.) \\
\midrule
TT-small & Wormhole Galaxy, 2$\times$4 submesh & 8$\times$12\,GB GDDR6 & 8$\times$86\,TFLOPS \\
         & 8 chips, FABRIC\_1D & 190\,GB/s per chip & BF16 (obs.) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The CPU and Wormhole devices share the same host machine (AMD EPYC 9354P, 32 cores, 566\,GB system RAM). The A100 is located in a separate server (Intel Xeon Icelake, 14 cores, 117\,GB RAM) connected over the same network; HuggingFace inference runs locally on that node. CPU throughput is measured on a single physical core of the EPYC host to isolate serial inference performance from parallelism.

\subsubsection{Software}

All Python code runs under Python 3.10.12. The Wormhole backend uses TTNN~0.67.0 (tt-metalium). The HuggingFace baselines use \texttt{transformers}~4.57.1 with PyTorch~2.3. The A100 node runs NVIDIA driver 595.45.04 with CUDA~13.2.

\subsubsection{Configurations}

Four configurations are evaluated and compared:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{TT-tiny}: Granite-4.0-h-tiny on 1$\times$4 Wormhole submesh (4 chips, fabric enabled). MoE weights in \texttt{bfloat8\_b}; all others \texttt{bfloat16}.
\item \textbf{TT-small}: Granite-4.0-h-small on 2$\times$4 Wormhole submesh (8 chips, fabric enabled). All weights \texttt{bfloat16}.
\item \textbf{CUDA-tiny / CUDA-small}: HuggingFace reference implementation on the NVIDIA A100 80\,GB GPU, \texttt{bfloat16}, via \texttt{transformers.AutoModelForCausalLM} with \texttt{device\_map="cuda"}.
\item \textbf{CPU-tiny / CPU-small}: Same HuggingFace reference, single CPU core on the host machine, \texttt{bfloat16}.
\end{itemize}

Decode throughput is measured over 18 steady-state steps (skipping step 1 as warmup) with a repetition-penalty sampler ($p=1.3$) to avoid degenerate token loops. All backends generate the same 20 tokens per prompt.

\subsection{Results}

\subsubsection{Decode throughput}

Table~\ref{tab:decode} reports decode throughput in tokens per second for all backends and prompt lengths.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Decode throughput (tok/s) across backends, models, and prompt lengths. Higher is better. A100 = NVIDIA A100 80\,GB; WH = Wormhole Galaxy submesh.}
\label{tab:decode}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
Model & Backend & short\_8 & short\_10 & med\_32 & long\_128 & long\_256 \\
       &          & (5 tok) & (8 tok) & (25 tok) & (96 tok) & (176 tok) \\
\midrule
tiny & CPU (1 core) & 2.58 & 2.60 & 2.61 & 2.61 & 2.62 \\
tiny & A100 GPU    & 7.65 & 9.40 & 9.32 & 9.34 & 9.19 \\
tiny & WH 1$\times$4  & 9.45 & 9.41 & 9.47 & 9.58 & 9.50 \\
\midrule
small & CPU (1 core) & 0.66 & 0.66 & 0.54 & 0.54 & 0.64 \\
small & A100 GPU    & 5.39 & 5.40 & 5.53 & 5.56 & 5.59 \\
small & WH 2$\times$4  & 3.71 & 3.71 & 3.71 & 3.71 & 3.74 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

For the tiny model, the Wormhole 1$\times$4 achieves 9.4--9.6 tok/s — matching or slightly exceeding the A100 (9.2--9.4 tok/s) at steady-state, and is $3.6\times$ faster than CPU. For the small model, the Wormhole 2$\times$4 achieves 3.7 tok/s vs.\ the A100's 5.4--5.6 tok/s ($1.5\times$ gap). Notably, the Wormhole decode rate is nearly constant across all prompt lengths, whereas the A100 shows a warm-up effect on very short prefills (short\_8 is slower due to CUDA kernel launch overhead dominating a 5-token sequence).

\subsubsection{Prefill throughput}

Table~\ref{tab:prefill} shows prefill throughput. Wormhole prefill is bottlenecked by the chunk-scan Mamba2 kernel; chunk size 256 was selected via the sweep in Table~\ref{tab:chunk} (Section~\ref{sec:chunk-tune}). The A100 benefits from PyTorch's highly-tuned batched scan implementation.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Prefill throughput (tok/s). Higher is better.}
\label{tab:prefill}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}
\toprule
Model & Backend & short\_10 & med\_32 & long\_128 & long\_256 \\
       &          & (8 tok) & (25 tok) & (96 tok) & (176 tok) \\
\midrule
tiny & CPU     &  0.71 &  2.17 &  8.22 & 14.85 \\
tiny & A100    & 25.38 & 75.12 & 279.1 & 503.2 \\
tiny & WH 1$\times$4 & 9.7 & 26.8 &  83.9 & 104.8 \\
\midrule
small & CPU     &  0.26 &  0.80 &  3.01 &  5.39 \\
