small & WH 2$\times$4 &  5.50 & 14.50 &  51.2 &  64.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Wormhole prefill throughput is $4.8\times$ slower than A100 at long sequences (tiny: 105 vs.\ 503 tok/s at 176 tokens; small: 64 vs.\ 178 tok/s). Both benefit from longer sequences due to the chunked-scan structure amortising setup cost.

\subsubsection{Model load time}

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Model load time (seconds). Lower is better.}
\label{tab:load}
\begin{tabular}{llr}
\toprule
Model & Backend & Load time (s) \\
\midrule
tiny & CPU     & 1.9 \\
tiny & A100    & 4.1 \\
tiny & WH 1$\times$4 & 99 \\
\midrule
small & CPU     & 23.6 \\
small & A100    & 175.5 \\
small & WH 2$\times$4 & 116 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Wormhole load time for tiny (99\,s) is dominated by uploading 64-expert weight matrices to 4 devices over PCIe. For small (116\,s), uploading 72-expert matrices to 8 devices is similarly bottlenecked, but is faster than the A100 (175\,s) because the A100 must quantise and re-pack weights via PyTorch's \texttt{bfloat16} conversion from float32 checkpoint shards.

\subsubsection{Output quality}
\label{sec:eval-quality}

The tiny model on Wormhole produces coherent, on-topic responses matching the A100 output for all five prompts (e.g., ``Paris. The Eiffel Tower...'' for the France prompt). The small model on Wormhole shows some garbled tokens at short prompts (e.g., ``Paris. The Eiffel\textbackslash nTowereight''), suggesting a numerical issue in the small-model logits path that was not present in the tiny model. The small model on A100 and CPU produces clean output. This correctness gap in the small TT implementation is an outstanding issue.

\subsection{Discussion}

\textbf{Decode gap vs.\ A100.}
The tiny model on 4 Wormhole chips (9.4--9.6 tok/s) matches or slightly exceeds a single A100 (9.2--9.4 tok/s). Given that the A100 benefits from a mature \texttt{triton} and CUDA Mamba2 kernel stack, and that Wormhole decode is currently fully dispatch-bound (Section~\ref{sec:bottleneck}), this is a strong result for early-stage hardware bring-up.

The small model gap is larger (3.7 vs.\ 5.5 tok/s, 33\%) despite using twice as many chips (8 vs.\ 1). The small model's per-layer weight matrices are $\sim$6$\times$ larger (H=4096 vs.\ 1536), making it more DRAM-bandwidth-bound. With dispatch overhead fixed via trace, the Wormhole system should approach or exceed the A100 for the small model, since 8 chips provide $8\times 190 = 1{,}520\,\text{GB/s}$ aggregate DRAM bandwidth vs.\ the A100 PCIe's $1{,}935\,\text{GB/s}$.

\textbf{CPU baselines.}
Both CPU baselines (2.6 tok/s tiny, 0.6 tok/s small) are far below Wormhole and A100, confirming that inference-time throughput requires hardware acceleration. The tiny CPU load time (1.9\,s) is fast because weights are small enough to fit in CPU cache-warm allocations; the small model (23.6\,s) requires paging several GB of weights.

\textbf{Constant decode rate on Wormhole.}
Unlike the A100 where decode throughput improves with context length (9.2 to 9.4 tok/s from short to long), Wormhole decode is flat at 9.4--9.6 tok/s for tiny regardless of prompt length. This is because the dominant cost is per-op PCIe dispatch, which is independent of sequence length. The A100 improvement at longer contexts reflects better CUDA kernel amortisation over longer KV-cache reads.

\textbf{Small model output quality.}
The garbled small-model responses on Wormhole (but not on CPU or A100) indicate a numerical issue specific to the TT path, likely in the logits gather or in the LM head shard composition across the 2$\times$4 mesh. The tiny model uses a different logits gather axis (dim=3 for vocab sharding) that was corrected in commit \texttt{8dae1dc}; the same fix may not be fully validated for the 2$\times$4 configuration. This is a correctness bug, not a performance issue, and does not affect the timing measurements.

\section{Threats to Validity}

Several factors may impact the validity of the results:
\begin{itemize}
\item Timing measurements are taken on a shared Galaxy system; other tenants' workloads may affect chip thermal state and PCIe bus utilisation.
\item Decode throughput on the 1$\times$4 Wormhole mesh varies between 9.4 and 9.6 tok/s across runs with identical code and a warm compiled-kernel cache; cold-cache runs (first run after \texttt{rm -rf generated/}) produce similar throughput once TTNN recompiles all kernels in the same session.
\item The trace deadlock is observed consistently but its firmware root cause has not been confirmed by the Metal team.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Ethics Considerations}

This work focuses on engineering and systems-level optimization of language models. No personal or sensitive data is used in this study. Energy efficiency is a motivating concern: Wormhole's dataflow architecture targets better FLOP/W than GPU systems for inference workloads, though this project does not measure power directly.

\section{Conclusion}

This project presents a complete port of Granite-4.0-h-tiny and Granite-4.0-h-small from HuggingFace to Tenstorrent Wormhole hardware using the TTNN Metal stack, and benchmarks the result against NVIDIA A100 PCIe and CPU baselines. The tiny model (1$\times$4 mesh) achieves 9.4--9.6 tok/s decode throughput — matching the A100 (9.2--9.4 tok/s) and $3.6\times$ faster than CPU. The small model (2$\times$4 mesh) reaches 3.7 tok/s vs.\ 5.5 tok/s on A100.

All measured throughput is obtained without Metal trace, meaning the system is dispatch-bound: 500+ individual PCIe commands per decode step contribute $\approx$50\,ms of the 130--145\,ms total step latency. The primary porting challenges encountered — including bfloat8\_b quantisation sensitivity, SSM state persistence across chunked prefill, MoE expert-parallel sharding, and CCL initialisation ordering — are documented in Section~\ref{sec:challenges} to guide future ports of hybrid SSM/attention models to similar hardware.

Chunk-size tuning (Section~\ref{sec:chunk-tune}) establishes that $C=256$ is the optimal prefill chunk size, achieving 140--145 tok/s prefill throughput vs.\ 41 tok/s at $C=192$. The fused \texttt{ssm\_update} Metal kernel (Section~\ref{sec:optimisation}) is designed and ready to deploy; it eliminates 4 dispatches per Mamba layer ($\sim$14\,ms saving per step) without any trace dependency.

The primary obstacle to full throughput is a deterministic deadlock between \texttt{ttnn.execute\_trace} and Ethernet fabric collectives (\texttt{all\_gather}): the Ethernet core is left in state \texttt{0x40} after trace replay, causing any subsequent collective to hang. Two paths forward exist: (a) running trace without fabric (no \texttt{all\_gather}, each device computes all experts) yields an estimated $\approx$50\,tok/s on the current mesh; (b) a firmware-level quiesce of Ethernet core state on trace exit would enable the full trace + expert-parallel combination, targeting $\approx$25\,tok/s on the 1$\times$4 mesh. The complete per-layer MLP trace implementation (Algorithms~\ref{alg:trace} and~\ref{alg:trace-fwd}) is ready to activate once either path is validated.

